<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simon Kimbangu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1887">1887</a>–<a href="/years/1951">1951</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simon Kimbangu was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a> religious leader in the Belgian Congo whose preaching and healing in 1921 drew large crowds. Belgian authorities saw his movement as a threat, arrested him and condemned him to death, a sentence commuted to life imprisonment. He died in prison at Élisabethville in 1951, and his followers founded the Kimbanguist Church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simon-kimbangu.jpg" alt="Simon Kimbangu" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simon_Kibangu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kimbangu was born at Nkamba, near Thysville. According to the Kimbanguist narrative, the traditional religious leader Papa Kuyela and Mama Luezi found the infant in the bush during a storm and adopted him, and his followers hold that his coming had been foretold in the 1600s by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimpa-vita">Kimpa Vita</a>, who was burnt alive for her message of the Holy Spirit and the liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. He became a Baptist and worked for several years as a catechist. Kimbanguist accounts add that he refused a call from Christ at first and sought work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Leopoldville</a> before returning to Nkamba in 1921.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His ministry of preaching and healing lasted from April to September 1921, and his disciples hold that he cured the sick, raised the dead and prophesied the liberation of black people. Bertram <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> holds that the Protestant and Catholic establishments appealed to the colonial authorities, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> van Reybrouck describes the Belgian administrator Léon Morel calling the missionaries to a meeting at Thysville, where the Catholics urged firm intervention and the Protestants a softer approach. Kimbangu and his followers were arrested on 6 June 1921, but he escaped into the bush with some disciples and his son Charles.</p><p>He went on preaching in hiding and gave himself up in September. A military court tried him without a lawyer and found him guilty of undermining public security and disturbing the peace. Asked by the Italian judge de Rossi to state his claims, he answered that he was not a prophet but the special envoy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, the Muanda Velela, the Holy Spirit in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kongo-language">Kikongo</a>. On 3 October 1921 he was sentenced to death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kimbangu died in prison at Elisabethville on 12 October 1951. In 1960 his body was exhumed and reburied at Nkamba with full military honours, and the Congolese government later made 6 April, the day his healing and prophetic work began, a holiday dedicated to the fight of Simon Kimbangu and the African consciousness.</p><p>After his trial the administration tried to suppress the movement: followers were banished across the country and their faith was outlawed, and in 1940 the leading exiles were put in guarded work camps under forced labour, where many died. The church spread underground and was recognised by the Belgian government in 1959. His son Joseph Diangienda, whom Kimbanguists regard as his second incarnation, organised it until his death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> on 8 July 1992, followed by his elder brother Salomon Dialungana Kiangani and then by Salomon's son Simon Kimbangu Kiangani at Nkamba.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Simon Kimbangu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Simon Kimbangu's</b> prophetic church was repressed.</blockquote><a href="/state/belgian-congo">Belgian Congo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nkamba, in what is now <a href="/countries/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. Died in Lubumbashi, in what is now <a href="/countries/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-5.03,14.59&amp;year=1928" aria-label="Nkamba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4955 2352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Simon Kimbangu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5405 2622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5405 2622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-5.03,14.59&amp;year=1928">What was here in 1928? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
